# What a confounder does
subtitle: "Coffee, smoking and heart disease in one causal diagram: how a common cause fakes a link, and how comparing like with like removes it."
tags: ["statistics","confounders","causal inference","study design","coffee smoking","epidemiology"]
teaches: ["concepts/confounding","methods/adjustment","concepts/causal-diagrams","methods/back-door-criterion"]
thumb: "card navy"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Pulcherrima | a forward, sharp epidemiologist; brisk and direct","take":"lines"}

## What a confounder does
sources:
    - id: dawber1974
      title: 'Coffee and cardiovascular disease: observations from the Framingham study'
      authors: Dawber, Kannel and Gordon
      year: 1974
      doi: 10.1056/NEJM197410242911703
      finding: Coffee's link to heart disease was carried by smoking

    box n_coffee "Coffee" #b5482e x 165 y 420 width 190 height 76 font_size 40 hidden true
    box n_heart "Heart disease" #2f6b8f x 825 y 420 width 250 height 76 font_size 36 hidden true
    box n_smoke "Smoking" #8a5fa8 cites ["dawber1974"] x 495 y 585 width 200 height 76 font_size 36 hidden true
    arrow e_ch n_coffee -> n_heart #3a3530 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    arrow e_sc n_smoke -> n_coffee #c0605a style.stroke_width 4.5 hidden true
    arrow e_sh n_smoke -> n_heart #c0605a style.stroke_width 4.5 hidden true
    label lab60 "60% vs 20%" above-left #c0605a attach_to e_sc font_size 30 hidden true
    math crude tex "\\text{all: } \\frac{14\\%}{8\\%} = 1.75" size 32 x 500 y 290 hidden true
    math strata tex "\\text{smokers: } \\frac{20\\%}{20\\%} = {1} \\qquad \\text{non-smokers: } \\frac{5\\%}{5\\%} = {1}" size 32 colors {"{1}":"#2f6b8f"} x 500 y 180 hidden true
    path fixr dashed #8a5fa8 points [[380,535],[610,535],[610,635],[380,635],[380,535]] hidden true
    text fixt "held fixed" #8a5fa8 x 700 y 600 font_size 28 hidden true
    group fixbox members ["fixr","fixt"] hidden true
    box n_bp "BP" dashed #8f887c x 495 y 420 width 90 height 56 font_size 30 style.opacity 0.7 hidden true
    arrow e_cb n_coffee -> n_bp dashed #8f887c hidden true
    arrow e_bh n_bp -> n_heart dashed #8f887c hidden true

    card title "What a confounder does"

How can coffee look linked to heart disease without causing it? That is what a confounder does, and here is what adjusting for it undoes.
    draw n_coffee n_heart parallel true

In a study, 14 percent of coffee drinkers get heart disease within ten years, against 8 percent of non-drinkers: a risk ratio of 1.75.
    draw crude

The tempting reading is this arrow: coffee raises the risk by three quarters.
    draw e_ch

But coffee drinkers smoke more. Here, 60 percent of them smoke, against 20 percent of non-drinkers.
    draw n_smoke e_sc lab60

Smoking goes with the coffee, and it causes heart disease. A common cause of both is what makes it a confounder.
    draw e_sh

Smokers bring their higher risk into the coffee group. That path, round the back through smoking, carries a link without any effect of coffee.
    flow e_sh

Adjusting means comparing like with like. Among smokers, 20 percent get heart disease, coffee or not. Among non-smokers, 5 percent.
    draw strata

Within each group the risk ratio is 1. The whole 1.75 came from mixing groups with different shares of smokers.
    highlight strata

    fade e_sc e_sh lab60 to 0.25

Holding smoking fixed closes the back door: within a group, smoking can no longer differ between drinkers and non-drinkers.
    draw fixbox

What remains is the arrow we set out to test, and in this example it was never there.
    erase e_ch

Coffee really was suspected of heart disease in the 1970s, until the Framingham study found the link was carried by smoking.
    highlight n_smoke

So adjust for a common cause like smoking: holding it fixed closes the back door it opens.
    highlight fixbox

    group bp members ["n_bp","e_cb","e_bh"]

    quiz "Suppose coffee raised blood pressure, and high blood pressure caused heart disease. Should the study adjust for blood pressure?"
        right: No: blood pressure would be a step on coffee's own path, so holding it fixed would block the very effect the study wants to measure.
        * Yes, adjust for every risk factor
        + No, it lies on coffee's own path
        * Only if it is measured well
---
